<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  CPS_Asymmetry: 64 entry colormap for HB, Hart's parameter B (the
  storm-relative thickness asymmetry). B is not a temperature, so it
  does not use the red/blue CPS_CoreDiverging map that HVTL, HVTU and
  HCPSidx share; red and blue mean warm core and cold core everywhere
  else in this set, and B in red read as "warm". Positive B (warm air on
  the right of the deep-layer flow in the Northern Hemisphere, the
  asymmetric or frontal configuration) ramps from pale magenta to deep
  purple, magenta being the hue Hart's own phase diagram uses for the
  asymmetric side. Negative B (warm air on the left, unusual, and a cue
  to check the steering direction) ramps from pale to dark teal, a hue
  that appears nowhere else in the set so the reversed case stands out.

  The alpha structure is identical to CPS_CoreDiverging: the middle 8
  entries (indices 28 through 35) are fully transparent, and alpha ramps
  0.2 to 0.8 over the next 4 entries on each side (24 to 27 and 36 to
  39). With the shipped HB range of -40 to +40 m each entry is 1.25 m,
  so B within 5 m of zero is transparent (symmetric), 5 to 10 m fades
  in, and the first fully opaque magenta step sits exactly at Hart's
  10 m onset line. Keep the range at +/-40 m to keep that alignment.

  File format: as CPS_CoreDiverging.cmap; VERIFY the element and
  attribute names against a .cmap file from your AWIPS build.
-->
<colorMap>
        <color r="0.000" g="0.380" b="0.400" a="1.0"/>
        <color r="0.018" g="0.395" b="0.415" a="1.0"/>
